@using Newtonsoft.Json;
@using WebVella.Erp.Web;
@using WebVella.Erp.Web.Models;
@using WebVella.Erp.Web.Utils;
@{
	var screenMessage = (ScreenMessage)ViewBag.ScreenMessage;
}
@if (screenMessage != null)
{
	@*  SECURITY - review finding L-OPEN-04, CWE-116 (improper encoding or escaping of output),
		OWASP A03:2021. THREAT ADDRESSED: these three values were interpolated with plain Razor
		expressions INSIDE QUOTED JAVASCRIPT STRING LITERALS, so they were HTML-encoded for a context that
		is not HTML. Two distinct defects followed, both confirmed in a browser rather than reasoned about.
		SCRIPT CORRUPTION. A BACKSLASH is the one character in this threat set that the HTML encoder does
		not touch at all. A message ending in one - "550 rejected by relay \" from a mail relay, say -
		emitted a literal backslash immediately before the closing quote, which escaped that quote and let
		the literal run on into the arguments after it. Chrome answers with a PARSE-TIME
		"Uncaught SyntaxError: missing ) after argument list" and discards THE WHOLE SCRIPT BLOCK, so
		neither the notification nor anything else in the block runs, with no trace outside the console.
		MANGLED DIAGNOSTICS. Everything else in the set is encoded, but into HTML entities - and script
		content is raw text in HTML, where an entity reference is NEVER decoded. So a quote reached the
		parser as the six characters "&quot;", a newline as "&#xA;", an apostrophe as "&#x27;" and a
		bullet as "&#x2022;". That is why a breakout was never demonstrated for those characters - they
		arrive inert - but it also means the operator was shown entity soup instead of the message. It is
		not hypothetical: the multi-line TLS failure text this platform stores in an email's server_error
		and shows through this very component rendered as
		"...connection.&#xA;&#xA;The server&#x27;s SSL certificate...".
		THE VALUES ARE NOW SERIALISED, not encoded. JsonConvert produces a complete JSON string literal -
		its own quotes included, backslashes doubled, quotes escaped, newlines emitted as the two-character
		escape a JavaScript parser expects - which
		is why the quotes are gone from the template below: supplying them would nest one string inside
		another. WvJsonRaw then escapes the one character JSON leaves alone that matters inside a script
		element, "<", so no value can spell "</script>" and end the block early. That is exactly the
		contract WvJsonRaw documents - a complete serialised JSON document written into script content on
		its own - and using it here needs no new helper and no new public API.
		The type label is serialised too. It comes from a four-value enum today and cannot carry a hostile
		character, but it is interpolated into the same statement, and leaving one interpolation encoded
		for the wrong context invites the next edit to copy it. *@
	@*  SECURITY - CWE-79 (stored cross-site scripting) and CWE-116 (improper encoding of output),
		OWASP A03:2021. THREAT ADDRESSED: correcting the encoding above delivers the value's TRUE characters
		into the notification library, and the library's sink parses what it is given as HTML - so the
		serialisation fix, on its own, converted text that had been inert as HTML entities into DOM that
		EXECUTES. Runtime-proven, not reasoned about: an SMTP peer's own response text reaching
		email.ServerError through SmtpInternalService.EmailSendNowOnPost rendered as a real <img> child of
		.toast-message whose onerror ran on the application's origin with the operator's authenticated
		session, and the report-only Content-Security-Policy observed it without blocking it.
		WHY escapeHtml AND WHY HERE. toastr 2.1.3 defaults escapeHtml to FALSE, and with that default BOTH
		arguments are raw: setTitle and setMessage each do $element.append(value), which parses markup.
		Setting it true routes both through the library's own escaper, and jQuery then decodes the
		references back to the original characters - so the operator still reads the real message, with the
		backslash and multi-line diagnostics this component was fixed to display correctly, while the
		parser can no longer be made to create an element. It is supplied in the per-call options object
		because toastr merges that object OVER its defaults (notify: $.extend(options, optionsOverride)),
		which closes the message and the title sinks together in the one file that owns this statement,
		without editing the vendored library or changing any other caller's behaviour.
		The values stay SERIALISED: escapeHtml protects the HTML context INSIDE the library, and the JSON
		string literal protects the JavaScript context of the statement below. Removing either reopens one
		of the two defects. *@
	var messageJson = Html.WvJsonRaw(JsonConvert.SerializeObject(screenMessage.Message ?? string.Empty));
	var titleJson = Html.WvJsonRaw(JsonConvert.SerializeObject(screenMessage.Title ?? string.Empty));
	var typeJson = Html.WvJsonRaw(JsonConvert.SerializeObject(screenMessage.Type.GetLabel()));
	<script>
	@if (screenMessage != null)
	{
		switch (screenMessage.Type)
		{
			case ScreenMessageType.Error:
				@:toastr["error"](@messageJson, @titleJson, {"escapeHtml": true,"positionClass": "toast-top-center","newestOnTop": false,"timeOut": 7000,"extendedTimeOut":7000});
				break;
			default:
				@:toastr[@typeJson](@messageJson, @titleJson, {"escapeHtml": true,"positionClass": "toast-top-center","newestOnTop": false,"timeOut": 3000,"extendedTimeOut":3000});
				break;
		}
	}
	</script>
}
